# MCP disconnect cadence and escalation threshold

The Minsky MCP server tracks disconnect, reconnect, and process-lifecycle
events for both the local stdio server and the hosted HTTP server. Operators
read the cadence via `mcp__minsky__debug_systemInfo` (under `mcpDisconnects`)
and the persisted JSONL log at
`~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json`.

> **Terminology (mt#2525).** Throughout this rule, **"session"** and
> **"disconnect"** mean an **MCP transport connection** — a single MCP server
> process's stdio (stdin/stdout) or hosted-HTTP connection lifecycle. This is
> the **transport** sense of "session" from the mt#2513 inventory; it is NOT a
> Minsky **workspace** session (the task-scoped clone created by `session_start`)
> and NOT a harness **conversation** (a Claude Code chat). In particular,
> `processRole: "main_session"` means an MCP connection that issued >= 1 tool
> call, and `escalation: "session"` is scoped to the current MCP server-process
> lifetime — neither refers to a workspace or a conversation.

## Cause classes

### FIRST: which topology is live? Classes 6 and 7 do not exist under the shim (mt#4720)

**Two entry forms write to this log, and only one of them produces the
proxy-observed classes below.** `src/mcp/setup/local-http-config.ts:44` models
them as distinct `EntryForm`s:

- **`mcp proxy`** — the stdio respawn proxy spawns `minsky mcp start` as a
  child and observes its exits. Produces `serverName: "minsky-proxy"` records:
  cause classes **6 and 7**.
- **`mcp shim`** — `minsky mcp shim --url <http>` bridges stdio to one
  long-lived HTTP daemon. **This is the current default (mt#4676, DONE).** No
  proxy process exists, so **no `minsky-proxy` record can ever be written.**

**Under the shim, the ABSENCE of class 6/7 records is not evidence about
crashes.** It is evidence about the topology. A grep for
`"serverName":"minsky-proxy"` returns zero whether or not anything died, which
is a probe that cannot fail (mem#704).

Check which form is live before reading anything into classes 6 and 7:

```bash
# The entry form, direct.
claude mcp list | grep -E '^minsky'      # `mcp shim --url …` or `mcp proxy …`

# Or ask the channel when it last wrote. An append-only log is a union over
# every writer that ever ran, so its HISTORY outlives the writer — "has it ever
# written?" passes on a retired channel. Compare the LAST write to your window.
grep -h '"serverName":"minsky-proxy"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq -r .timestamp | sort | tail -1
```

On the principal's machine, measured 2026-08-29: 406 proxy records spanning
`2026-07-21T07:33:13Z` → `2026-08-22T07:11:38Z`, then nothing. The writer was
retired with the shim migration; the records remain and stay readable, which is
why classes 6 and 7 are scoped below rather than deleted.

**What observes an uncatchable death under the shim: nothing (verified, mt#4720).**
`src/mcp/shim/**` contains no disconnect-recording code at all — the only writers
in `src/mcp/` are `src/mcp/server.ts` (the daemon self-reporting) and
`src/mcp/stdio-proxy/proxy.ts` (not in this path). `src/mcp/server.ts` installs
handlers for
**SIGTERM / SIGINT / SIGHUP only**, all catchable; SIGKILL, an OOM kill, or a
runtime panic gives it no opportunity to record anything. So on the shim
topology a hard daemon death leaves **no disconnect row of any cause**. The only
residual traces are a subsequent `process_start` row from the replacement
daemon, and, client-side, a transient
`minsky mcp shim: daemon request failed: The socket connection was closed
unexpectedly` surfaced to the caller (mem#1273). Diagnose a suspected hard death
from an unexplained `process_start`, not from the absence of a cause row.

A single `stdin_close` reading would conflate four real-world classes that
the tracker now distinguishes (mt#1682):

1. **Harness-driven cycling** — Claude Code spawns short-lived MCP server
   processes for hooks and pre-flight probes. Recorded as
   `cause: "stdin_close"` with `uptimeMs < 5_000`. **Excluded from
   escalation** as a normal harness lifecycle pattern.
2. **Server-initiated `staleness_exit`** — mt#1315's stale-source mechanism
   intentionally exits the process so the next call gets a fresh server
   loaded from the new HEAD. Recorded as `cause: "staleness_exit"`.
   **Excluded from escalation** as by-design behavior.
3. **Genuine long-lived MCP-connection closure** — the harness closes an MCP
   transport connection that had been doing real work. Recorded as
   `cause: "stdin_close"` with
   `uptimeMs >= 5_000` AND `processRole: "main_session"`. **Counts toward
   escalation** — this is the user-visible reliability concern.
4. **Signal-driven shutdowns** — process received SIGTERM, SIGINT, or
   SIGHUP. Recorded as `signal_sigterm` / `signal_sigint` / `signal_sighup`.
   **Excluded from escalation** as by-design behavior.
5. **Stdout pollution** — terminal escape codes, build progress text, and
   status emoji from subprocesses leaking onto the MCP stdio's stdout.
   Currently tolerated by Claude Code (logged as "Ignoring non-JSON line on
   stdout") and is **not a disconnect cause** but is a framing-corruption
   hazard worth tracking. Cleanup is a separate concern.
6. **Proxy-observed exits (mt#2830)** — **`mcp proxy` topology only; produces no
   records under the `mcp shim` default — see §FIRST above.** The stdio respawn proxy
   (`src/mcp/stdio-proxy/proxy.ts`) is a SEPARATE OS process from the inner
   server and is therefore the only reliable observer of exits the inner
   cannot self-report — most importantly **SIGKILL**, which is uncatchable
   and gives the inner zero opportunity to run any recording code before it
   dies (previously: no disconnect-log entry at all for that death, not even
   `"unknown"`). Recorded as `cause: "signal_sigkill"` for SIGKILL,
   `cause: "proxy_observed_crash"` for a non-zero exit with no signal, or the
   existing `signal_sigterm`/`signal_sigint`/`signal_sighup` causes for those
   specific signals — under a **distinct `serverName: "minsky-proxy"`**
   bucket, so historical cause-distribution stats for the inner server
   (`"Minsky MCP Server"` / `"minsky-hosted"`) are unaffected. Routine clean
   exits (code 0, no signal — the common `staleness_exit` case) are
   deliberately NOT duplicated here; the inner already records those with a
   more specific cause. **Escalation treatment splits by cause, and the split
   is not obvious from this paragraph (corrected mt#4481):**
   `signal_sigkill` and `proxy_observed_crash` **count toward escalation** —
   they are absent from `SERVER_INITIATED_CAUSES`, and an external actor
   caused them. But `signal_sigterm` / `signal_sigint` / `signal_sighup` are
   **excluded** even when the proxy is the one recording them, because the
   escalation predicate keys on the CAUSE, not on which process observed it,
   and class 4 already excludes those three. This paragraph read as a blanket
   "counts toward escalation" until mt#4481, which was right for two of its
   five causes.
7. **Signals with no dedicated taxonomy entry (mt#4481)** — **`mcp proxy`
   topology only, like class 6 — see §FIRST above.** The bare
   `cause: "signal"`, emitted by `classifyExitForDisconnectLog`'s `default:`
   branch (`src/mcp/stdio-proxy/proxy.ts`) for any signal outside
   SIGKILL/SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP: SIGBUS, SIGSEGV, SIGABRT, SIGTRAP, and so
   on. Like class 6 it is proxy-observed and carries
   `serverName: "minsky-proxy"`. **Read `event.signal` for the actual signal
   name** — this generic bucket exists so these do not collapse into
   `"unknown"`, and the raw name is always recorded alongside.
   **COUNTS toward escalation as of mt#4499** — these are runtime crashes, and
   surfacing them is what the escalation signal is for. It was EXCLUDED until
   then: `"signal"` sat in `SERVER_INITIATED_CAUSES`, placed there on
   2026-05-08 by mt#1682 when the cause was a dormant backward-compat
   placeholder with no producer and "a signal" meant SIGTERM / SIGINT /
   SIGHUP. mt#2830 then adopted the dormant value for CRASH signals; the
   meaning changed and the membership did not. Removing it reclassified no
   historical record — no record ever carried the old meaning. Worth opening
   when it appears: on the
   principal's machine every one of the 156 such records was a runtime crash
   of the inner server, including Bun panics whose `stderrTail` carries the
   full crash report. This cause has been emitted since mt#2830 but went
   undocumented here until mt#4481, so a reader classifying a cause
   distribution had no entry for roughly 2% of it.

## Staleness-exit drain semantics (mt#2701, mt#2830)

`triggerStaleSignal` (`src/mcp/server.ts`) does not exit immediately once
staleness is detected. It tracks an in-flight request counter and defers
`process.exit(0)` until the first IDLE GAP (counter reaches 0), bounded by a
hard cap (`staleDrainCapMs`, default 30s) so a continuously-busy server
cannot postpone the exit indefinitely.

**mt#2830 fix:** requests that ARRIVE during the drain window — after
staleness was detected but before the process actually exits — are served
normally on the still-loaded code, not rejected. Before this fix, the same
`draining` flag used by true graceful shutdown (`drain()`, the SIGTERM/SIGINT
path) was also set by staleness-exit, so any new call landing in that window
was rejected with `Error("Server is shutting down")` — surfacing to MCP
clients as **error -32603** even though the server had not yet decided to
die. A dedicated `pendingStaleExit` flag now carries the "exit once idle"
intent without gating new admissions; `draining` is reserved for genuine
shutdown. The freshness guarantee is unchanged for calls made AFTER the
process actually exits and is respawned by the stdio proxy — only the
in-between window changed behavior.

## Diagnostic fields (mt#2830)

Proxy-observed `disconnect` events additionally carry, when available:

- `exitCode` — the child's exit code (`null` when killed by a signal)
- `signal` — the raw POSIX signal name (e.g. `"SIGKILL"`), independent of
  whether `cause` has a dedicated taxonomy entry for it
- `stderrTail` — a bounded tail (last ~4000 chars) of the child's stderr
  output captured immediately before it closed
- `lastTransportEvent` — the last JSON-RPC line observed on the outbound
  path (child → client) before the close, truncated to ~500 chars — a
  breadcrumb for "what was the server doing when it died"

These are absent on inner-server-recorded events (the inner has no stderr or
exit-code visibility into itself) and on proxy events where the corresponding
signal wasn't available.

## Process-role classification (mt#1705)

Even after filtering class 1 (uptimeMs < 5s), some "helper" processes
(hooks spawning `minsky` CLI, /mcp reconnect probes, pre-flight harness
checks) linger 33s–300s before closing. These looked like class 3 (genuine
session closures) under the uptime-only filter. The `processRole` field
discriminates them using the tool-call count at disconnect time:

- **`"helper"`** — `processRole: "helper"` — 0 tool calls before disconnect.
  The process connected but never invoked a tool. This covers hooks, probes,
  and pre-flight checks. **Excluded from escalation** regardless of uptime.
- **`"main_session"`** — `processRole: "main_session"` — 1+ tool calls before
  disconnect. Substantive working session. Still subject to cause-based and
  uptime-based escalation filters.

The discriminating signal is the tool-call count (incremented by
`incrementToolCallCount()` in `src/mcp/server.ts`'s `CallToolRequestSchema` handler
before each tool dispatch). Helper processes characteristically connect without
invoking tools; this is the only reliable signal that doesn't overlap with
short working sessions.

**Legacy events** without `processRole` (from pre-mt#1705 logs) are surfaced
in their own `byRole.legacy` aggregate bucket so operators can see migration
progress (the legacy count shrinks toward 0 as new-format events saturate the
log). For escalation eligibility, legacy events are treated conservatively as
escalation-eligible — same as `"main_session"` — because we have no tool-call
count to discriminate them. The aggregate split is informational; only the
predicate's conservative treatment matters for the escalation signal.

## Recurrence-threshold escalation

The tracker emits `escalation: "none" | "session" | "daily"` in the
`debug.systemInfo` payload, computed from the in-memory event list:

- `daily` fires when **escalation-eligible** disconnects in the last 24h
  exceed 3.
- `session` fires when escalation-eligible disconnects in the current
  server-process lifetime exceed 1.

"Escalation-eligible" means ALL of:
- `kind: "disconnect"`
- `cause ∉ {staleness_exit, signal_sigterm, signal_sigint, signal_sighup, server_close, idle_timeout}`
  — this list must match `SERVER_INITIATED_CAUSES` in
  **`src/mcp/disconnect-escalation.ts`** exactly, and
  `disconnect-tracker.test.ts` asserts it. Note `signal_sigkill`,
  `proxy_observed_crash` and (since mt#4499) the bare `signal` are deliberately
  NOT in the set — they DO escalate.
  *History, corrected:* mt#4481 said "the bare `signal` was added to the code by
  mt#2830 and to this list by mt#4481." Wrong on the first half — `git blame`
  puts it in the set on 2026-05-08 via mt#1682 (`32283680c7`), two months before
  mt#2830 gave the cause a producer. mt#4499 removed it and moved the
  definition out of `disconnect-tracker.ts` into a dependency-free module,
  because a THIRD hand-copy in `src/cockpit/widgets/s3-gauges.ts` had been
  disagreeing with both.
- `uptimeMs >= 5_000` (or `uptimeMs` absent — legacy mt#1645 events without
  uptime are counted conservatively)
- `processRole !== "helper"` (mt#1705 — helper sessions never escalate;
  legacy events without `processRole` are counted conservatively as
  escalation-eligible and are surfaced in `byRole.legacy` separately from
  `byRole.main_session`)

When `escalation` is non-`none`, file or update the structural-fix follow-up
task. The threshold is calibrated against the empirically observed cadence
(2026-05-08: ~7 disconnects in 4 hours, of which most were class 1 / 2 /
4 — only class 3 events drive the eligible count).

## Reading the persisted log

The log file is **append-only JSONL**: one JSON object per line, no
enclosing array. Each line carries:

- `timestamp`, `serverName`, `kind`, `cause` (always)
- `uptimeMs` on `disconnect` and `transport_error` events
- `processRole` on `disconnect` events (mt#1705): `"helper"` or `"main_session"`
- `pid` on `process_start` events
- `error` on `transport_error` and (optionally) `disconnect` events
- `exitCode`, `signal`, `stderrTail`, `lastTransportEvent` on
  `serverName: "minsky-proxy"` `disconnect` events (mt#2830) — see
  "Diagnostic fields" above

The legacy mt#1645 single-array JSON format is still readable on load
(backward-compat migration). After upgrade, new events append as JSONL
lines after the legacy array; both halves load on the next restart.

### The legacy seam, and why a zero-row read is not silence (mt#4481)

A log that predates the mt#1682 migration has a **hybrid** shape: a
pretty-printed `[ … ]` array, then JSONL. Until mt#4481 the two halves were
GLUED — the mt#1645 writer ended its file with `]` and no trailing newline,
and `appendEvent` wrote only a trailing one, so the first appended record
landed on the same line as the closing bracket:

```
]{"timestamp":"…","serverName":"…","kind":"process_start","cause":"process_start","pid":91211}
```

`appendEvent` now writes a leading newline when the file does not already end
in one, so no new seam can form — but **a file that already carries one keeps
it**, and the tracker's own loader tolerates it, so nothing repairs it for you.

**What it breaks, and only this:** `jq` aborts a stream at its first parse
error, and the seam line is the FIRST line a `"kind":"process_start"` grep
matches (the legacy array is pretty-printed, so `"kind": "process_start"` with
a space never matches the compact pattern). So the *restart* query — and no
other — dies with `jq: parse error: Unmatched ']'` at **exit 5, emitting zero
rows**. The `disconnect` recipes below are unaffected because that line is not
a `disconnect`.

**Do not read that zero as "the tracker stopped recording."** It is the shape
that produced exactly that misreading on 2026-08-23, costing a task filed
against a healthy subsystem. Two ways to tell in one command each:

```bash
# Is there a seam? Exactly one hit means yes; zero means the file is clean.
grep -c '^\]' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log.json

# Read it anyway: jq's slurp parser treats `]{` as two documents, so this
# works with or without a seam. Slower (loads the whole file), always correct.
jq -s -r '[.[] | if type=="array" then .[] else . end]
  | map(select(type=="object" and .kind=="process_start"))
  | sort_by(.timestamp) | .[-5:] | .[] | "\(.timestamp) pid=\(.pid)"' \
  ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log.json
```

To repair a seamed file in place — splitting the line, changing nothing else:

```bash
perl -0pi -e 's/\]\{/]\n{/' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log.json
```

Confirm with `jq -s length` before and after; the counts must match.

### The hybrid itself is retired on this host (mt#4558)

**This host's log is now uniform JSONL** — the legacy 65-record array was
converted by `scripts/normalize-disconnect-log.ts`, so every non-blank line
parses on its own and every recipe below works with no caveat. The seam
described above is history HERE.

**Everything above is kept anyway, because it is not history everywhere.**
Another machine, a restored backup, or an old `~/.local/state/minsky` will
still present the hybrid, and all four readers still handle it — the tracker's
`loadFromDisk`, `disconnect-event-sweep.ts`, the cockpit's `s3-gauges.ts`
widget, and `measure-escalation-blast-radius.ts`. The section above is how you
RECOGNISE one; this section is how you retire it:

```bash
bun scripts/normalize-disconnect-log.ts              # preview: counts, no writes
bun scripts/normalize-disconnect-log.ts --execute    # backup, convert, verify
```

It is a no-op on an already-uniform file, so it is safe to run blind.

## The log is segmented by month, and the recipes glob (mt#4495)

The corpus is no longer one file. The ACTIVE file keeps its exact name and path;
completed months sit beside it:

```
mcp-disconnect-log.json           <- active, still the path every writer appends to
mcp-disconnect-log-2026-07.json   <- one segment per calendar month, never deleted
mcp-disconnect-log-2026-08.json
```

**Every census recipe below therefore globs — `mcp-disconnect-log*.json`.** Three
properties make that a one-character change rather than a rewrite, and each was
verified rather than assumed:

- The recipes already use `grep -h`, which suppresses filename prefixes. That
  flag was there for readability and turns out to be exactly what multi-file
  needs.
- **Shell expansion is already chronological, with the active file LAST.** `-`
  (0x2D) sorts before `.` (0x2E), so `…log-2026-05.json` … `…log-2026-12.json`
  precede `…log.json`. Nothing needs sorting.
- The glob requires a `.json` suffix, so it excludes the normalizer's backups
  (`…log.json.backup-<iso>`) and the sweep's `mcp-disconnect-sweep-hwm.json`.
  Both would otherwise corrupt a census — the backup by double-counting.

**A recipe that reads only `mcp-disconnect-log.json` now answers a question about
the CURRENT MONTH, not about history.** That is the failure mode worth naming: it
returns a clean, plausible number with no error, and the narrowing is invisible
unless you knew to look. On the first day of a month it can approach zero. If you
mean history, glob.

The seam-detection and in-place repair commands above deliberately do NOT glob:
`perl -0pi` rewrites one file, and `grep -c` over a glob prints per-file counts
instead of one number. Run them per-segment if a hybrid ever turns up in one.

**Retention: nothing is deleted, ever.** The decision and its measurement are in
mt#4495. The short version is that the `system_events` projection of this log is
not a substitute for it — 48% of the records, missing the first 57 days, and
`kind` absent from every row (mt#4617) — so a deleted segment is unrecoverable.

**Check uniformity with a line-by-line parse, never `jq -s`.** Slurp SUCCEEDS
on a hybrid — that is how mt#4481 confirmed record counts across the repair —
so it reports a clean pass on precisely the state you are trying to detect. A
probe that cannot fail is not verification:

```bash
# Non-parsing lines. 0 = uniform. A pretty-printed array head shows ~392.
awk 'NF' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | while IFS= read -r l; do printf '%s' "$l" | jq -e . >/dev/null 2>&1 || echo BAD; done | wc -l
```

Quick consumer commands:

```bash
# Cause distribution today
grep -h '"kind":"disconnect"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq -r '.cause' | sort | uniq -c

# Role distribution today (mt#1705)
grep -h '"kind":"disconnect"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq -r '.processRole // "legacy"' | sort | uniq -c

# Eligible-only count (main_session long-lived harness closures)
grep -h '"kind":"disconnect"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq 'select(.uptimeMs >= 5000 and .cause == "stdin_close" and .processRole == "main_session")'

# Process count today
grep -c '"kind":"process_start"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json

# Most recent restarts (timestamp + pid) — the "why did the daemon go down?" query.
# Correlate the newest pid against `minsky mcp status` to confirm it is the live one.
# NOTE: this is the recipe the seam breaks. If it exits 5 with `Unmatched ']'`,
# the file has a seam — see "The legacy seam" above; do NOT read it as silence.
grep -h '"kind":"process_start"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq -r '"\(.timestamp) pid=\(.pid)"' | tail -5

# Restarts per day — the input to a cadence question (mt#4487). Report the
# per-day counts, never a pooled average: this cadence is strongly bimodal
# (quiet periods vs active src/ merging) and a mean describes neither regime.
grep -h '"kind":"process_start"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq -r '.timestamp[0:10]' | sort | uniq -c

# Proxy-observed exits with full diagnostics (mt#2830) — SIGKILL/crash cases
grep -h '"serverName":"minsky-proxy"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq '{timestamp, cause, exitCode, signal, stderrTail, lastTransportEvent}'

# Unknown-cause share (the mt#2830 gap-analysis metric)
grep -h '"kind":"disconnect"' ~/.local/state/minsky/mcp-disconnect-log*.json \
  | jq -r '.cause' | sort | uniq -c | awk '$2=="unknown"{u=$1} {t+=$1} END{printf "%d/%d (%.1f%%)\n", u+0, t, 100*(u+0)/t}'
```

## `unknown` on an HTTP session is a protocol default, not a classification gap (mt#4511)

Before re-filing "most disconnects are unclassified", read this section — mt#4511 was filed on
exactly that premise and the premise was wrong.

**Every recipe above scopes itself to `"kind":"disconnect"`, and that is load-bearing.** A cause
answers "why did a connection END?", so it is meaningful only for an ending. Until mt#4511
`debug_systemInfo`'s `mcpDisconnects.byCause` did NOT apply that filter — it counted every event
kind, including `reconnect` (whose cause defaulted to `unknown`) and `process_start` (whose cause
repeats its kind). So the tool surface and these recipes answered the same question differently,
and the tool's answer was the misleading one. Measured 2026-08-25T00:58Z: `byCause` reported 235
of 289 events `unknown` (81%), of which **183 were reconnects**; over disconnects alone the figure
was 56 of 86 (65%). `byCause` now filters like `byRole` and agrees with these recipes; reconnects
now record the tautological cause `reconnect`.

**Of the disconnects that remain `unknown`, most are HTTP sessions closing normally.**
`src/mcp/server.ts` wires every per-session HTTP `Server` with `"unknown"` as its default cause, because
the transport close event does not distinguish client-initiated from server-initiated closes at
the protocol level. That is a property of the protocol, not a gap in this taxonomy — adding cause
markers cannot reduce it. Treat a large `unknown` share as expected for HTTP and interrogate it
only when the DISCONNECT COUNT itself moves.

**`uptimeMs` on a disconnect is the SESSION's lifetime, not the process's (mt#4511).** They
coincide for stdio (one process binds 1:1 with one session) and diverge sharply for HTTP, where
many short sessions live inside one long-running daemon. Before mt#4511 it was always process
uptime, so the `uptimeMs >= 5000` leg of the eligibility predicate — the one that exists to
exclude short-lived probes — could not fire for any HTTP session, and routine ~60s poll churn
counted toward escalation. That is why escalation sat at `daily`: not a reliability regression,
but per-minute poll sessions each reporting the daemon's multi-hour age. When reading events
recorded BEFORE mt#4511, remember their `uptimeMs` is process age and cannot tell you how long
the session lived.

Consequence for the `Eligible-only count` recipe above: it hard-codes `.cause == "stdin_close"`,
so it describes the stdio population and silently excludes HTTP sessions entirely. That is a
narrower question than `escalation` answers — do not read the two as the same number.

### The ~60s runs are distinct poll sessions, not one process re-recording (mt#4511)

You will see runs of `unknown` disconnects spaced **exactly 60s apart, with `uptimeMs`
incrementing by exactly 60,000 ms**. Measured 2026-08-25: 20 of 56 unclassified disconnects sat in
5 such runs, the longest 7 events (00:38:13Z → 00:44:13Z). It reads like one live process emitting
a disconnect per minute. **It is not — do not re-open this as a duplicate-recording bug.**

`processRole` is the discriminator, and it is decisive. `recordDisconnect` EVICTS the session's
tool-call count, so a single session disconnecting repeatedly would classify as `helper` on every
event after the first. The observed sequences do the opposite:

```
run of 3: main_session -> main_session -> main_session
run of 4: helper -> main_session -> main_session -> main_session
run of 7: main_session -> main_session -> main_session -> main_session -> helper -> main_session -> helper
```

**12 of the 15 non-first events are `main_session`**, which is only possible if a DISTINCT session
made its own tool call after the previous eviction. These are separate HTTP sessions on a ~60s
client poll cadence.

The exactly-60,000 ms uptime delta was itself the pre-mt#4511 bug, not evidence of a loop: every
session stamped the DAEMON's age, so N sessions 60s apart in one daemon necessarily showed uptimes
60s apart. Post-mt#4511 these same sessions report their own short lifetimes and drop out of
escalation. Reproduce the check by grouping `kind=="disconnect"` events on `(gap ≈ 60s AND uptime
delta ≈ 60s)` and reading the `processRole` sequence within each run.

